\clearpage

\subsection{School Board Vote Roll Off}

Next, I examine the same models above applied to voter rolloff in the spring 
elections. I explore whether any of the models above explain the difference between 
top ticket turnout and school board turnout. As much as undervote coincides with 
political relevance of the school board race to the electorate, the relationships 
observed above shouldalso be observed in the measure of undervote. 


<<cleanundervotedata, results='hide'>>=
load("../../data/cache/springElectionVotes.rda")
# load("data/cache/springElectionVotes.rda")
springVotes$votesTopTicketSpring <- springVotes$VotesCast
springVotes$VotesCast <- NULL
dist_turn <- merge(dist_turn, springVotes, 
                   by = c("distid", "year"), all.x = TRUE)

dist_turn$topTicketTurnoutSpring <- dist_turn$votesTopTicketSpring / dist_turn$VAP_adj
dist_turn$turnoutDiff <- dist_turn$topTicketTurnoutSpring - dist_turn$turnout

vars2 <- c("turnout", "turnoutLag2", "year", "fallTurnout", "incumbent",
                        "VAP_adjLOG", "distid", "per_white_all", "per65o", 
                        "PerBachelorOrAbove", "OOH_share", "median_incomeLOG", 
                        "teachShareofVoters", "districtwide", 
                        "contest", "minBlaisLago")

vars1 <- c("turnout", "turnoutLag2", "year", "fallTurnout", "topTicketTurnoutSpring",
           "turnoutDiff", "ImputedFlag",
                        "VAP_adjLOG", "distid", "per_white_all", "per65o", 
                        "PerBachelorOrAbove", "OOH_share", "median_incomeLOG", 
                        "fallTwoPartyShareDem", "fallPartyDivision", 
                        "districtwide", "contest", "minBlaisLago")

moddat <- dist_turn[, unique(c(vars1, vars2))]
moddat$ImputedFlag[is.na(moddat$ImputedFlag)] <- "Observed"
moddat$ImputedFlag <- defac(moddat$ImputedFlag)
moddat$ImputedFlag[moddat$ImputedFlag == "Imputed2010"] <- "Imputed"
moddat$ImputedFlag[moddat$ImputedFlag == "Observed2010"] <- "Observed"
moddat$ImputedFlag <- factor(moddat$ImputedFlag, levels = c("Imputed", "Observed"))
moddat$ImputedFlag <- relevel(moddat$ImputedFlag, ref = "Imputed")
moddat$VAP_adjLOG_sq <- moddat$VAP_adjLOG^2
moddat <- na.omit(moddat)
moddat <- moddat[order(moddat$distid, moddat$year),]
moddat$distid <- factor(moddat$distid)
moddat$year <- factor(moddat$year)
moddat$districtwide <- factor(moddat$districtwide)
moddat$turnout <- moddat$turnout * 100
moddat$turnoutDiff <- moddat$turnoutDiff * 100
# moddat$turnoutLag2 <- moddat$turnoutLag2 * 100
# moddat$turnoutLag1 <- moddat$turnoutLag1 * 100
# moddat$per_white_all <- moddat$per_white_all * 100
# moddat$per65o <- moddat$per65o * 100
# moddat$PerBachelorOrAbove <- moddat$PerBachelorOrAbove * 100
# moddat$OOH_share <- moddat$OOH_share * 100
# moddat$fallTurnout <- moddat$fallTurnout * 100
# moddat$VAP_adj <- log(moddat$VAP_adj)
# moddat$median_income <- log(moddat$median_income)
centr <- mean(moddat$turnoutDiff)
stddev <- sd(moddat$turnoutDiff)
moddat$outflag <- (moddat$turnoutDiff - centr) / stddev
moddat <- moddat[moddat$outflag <= 5 & moddat$outflag >= -5,]
nums <- sapply(moddat, is.numeric)
ScaleMeans <- apply(moddat[, nums], 2, mean, na.rm=TRUE)
ScaleSD <- apply(moddat[, nums], 2, sd, na.rm=TRUE)
moddatScaleUndervote <- cbind(ScaleMeans, ScaleSD); rm(ScaleMeans, ScaleSD)
nums <- sapply(moddat, is.numeric)
moddat[, nums] <- apply(moddat[, nums], 2, rescale)
dd <<- datadist(moddat)
options(datadist="dd")
@

For undervote data, I use the spring elections in from 2007-2012. To calculate 
rolloff, I take the difference in the turnout for the top ticket in the spring 
election and the turnout for the highest turnout school board election in the 
school district. The greater this number, the greater the share of voters who 
failed to complete their ballots. The median district has \Sexpr{iPer(mean(dist_turn$turnoutDiff, na.rm=TRUE)*100)} percentage point difference in their turnout, meaning that if the turnout in the school board election was 1,810, then the turnout for the 
top ticket race would be \Sexpr{iNum2(1810 + 1810*mean(dist_turn$turnoutDiff, na.rm=TRUE))}. Thus ballot fatigue is not that uncommon and matches findings in other 
elections elsewhere \citep{streb09,wattenberg00}.


\begin{tiny}
<<undervoteMMfits1, results='asis'>>=
undervoteMMlag <- lmer(turnoutDiff ~ turnoutLag2 + fallTurnout + 
                         VAP_adjLOG + VAP_adjLOG_sq + per_white_all + 
                         per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove + OOH_share + 
                         median_incomeLOG + districtwide + incumbent +
                         ImputedFlag + minBlaisLago + 
                         (1|year) + (1|distid), 
              data = moddat, REML = FALSE)
undervoteMMclose <- lmer(turnoutDiff ~  minBlaisLago + VAP_adjLOG + fallTurnout +
                           VAP_adjLOG_sq + per_white_all + 
                    per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove + OOH_share + 
                      ImputedFlag + minBlaisLago * VAP_adjLOG +
                    median_incomeLOG + districtwide + incumbent +
                    (1|year) + (1|distid), 
              data = moddat, REML = TRUE)
undervoteMMcloseB <- lmer(turnoutDiff ~ VAP_adjLOG +  VAP_adjLOG_sq + 
                     fallTurnout +
                     VAP_adjLOG + per_white_all + 
                     per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove + OOH_share + 
                       ImputedFlag +
                     median_incomeLOG + districtwide + contest + incumbent + 
                       contest * VAP_adjLOG + 
                   (1|year) + (1|distid), 
              data = moddat, REML = TRUE)
undervoteMMteach <- lmer(turnoutDiff ~ teachShareofVoters + fallTurnout + 
                           minBlaisLago + VAP_adjLOG + VAP_adjLOG_sq +
                           per_white_all + per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove +
                  OOH_share + median_incomeLOG + districtwide + incumbent +
                    ImputedFlag+  
                   teachShareofVoters * VAP_adjLOG + (1|year) + (1|distid), 
              data = moddat, REML = TRUE)
undervoteMMparty <- lmer(turnoutDiff ~ fallTwoPartyShareDem*VAP_adjLOG + VAP_adjLOG_sq +
                           fallTurnout + minBlaisLago +
                  VAP_adjLOG + per_white_all + per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove +
                   OOH_share + median_incomeLOG + districtwide + incumbent +
                    ImputedFlag + 
                      (1|year) + (1|distid), 
              data = moddat, REML = TRUE)
undervoteMMpartyB <- lmer(turnoutDiff ~ fallPartyDivision*VAP_adjLOG +  VAP_adjLOG_sq+ 
                           fallTurnout + minBlaisLago +
                  VAP_adjLOG + per_white_all + per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove +
                   OOH_share + median_incomeLOG + districtwide +incumbent +
                    ImputedFlag +
                      (1|year) + (1|distid), 
              data = moddat, REML = TRUE)

coefNamesUnd1 <- c("Intercept", "Turnout Lag (2)","Fall Turnout", 
                 "ln(VAP)", "$ln(VAP)^{2}$","\\% White", 
                  "\\% Over 65", "\\% Bach. +", "\\% OOH", 
                  "ln(MedianIncome)", "District-wide", "No Incumbent","Imputed",
                 "Blais-Lago", "Blais-Lago x VAP", 
                 "Uncontested Seat", "Uncon. Seat. x VAP")


apsrtable(undervoteMMlag, undervoteMMclose,  undervoteMMcloseB,
          caption = "Partial Pooling Model of School Board Undervote",
           model.names = c("Lag", "Close Race", "Close Race 2"),
            label = "tab:meUndv1", notes = list(stars.note), 
          Sweave = TRUE, 
          coef.names = coefNamesUnd1)
@
\end{tiny}

Table \ref{tab:meUndv1} shows the results of the first three types of models investigated 
above for turnout. Note that here the sign is flipped -- the smaller the dependent 
variable, the more turnout for school board in relation to the top ticket turnout 
in the spring election. Prior turnout continues to matter in the lagged model, 
the higher the prior turnout the less the gap between top ticket and school board 
races in the present spring election. In the measures of the closeness of seats 
the less competitive a race is the greater the gap between the top ticket and the 
board race -- but this effect is only in interaction with larger district sizes. 
In small communities this relationship does not matter. This could be for a couple 
of reasons -- information costs to voters are less in small communities meaning 
voters reaching the polls in the spring election are more likely to feel informed 
enough to complete the ballot. Another reason could be that in large communities 
school boards are even further down the ballot due to more county and municipality 
elections and voters get tired out more quickly unless the race is likely competitive 
and then they persevere. 

\begin{tiny}
<<undervoteMMfits2, results='asis'>>=
coefNamesUnd2 <- c("Intercept", "Teacher Share VAP", "Fall Turnout", 
                   "Blais-Lago", "ln(VAP)", "$ln(VAP)^{2}$","\\% White", 
                  "\\% Over 65", "\\% Bach. +", "\\% OOH", 
                  "ln(MedianIncome)", "District-wide", "No Incumbent","Imputed", 
                 "Teacher Share x VAP", "Fall Dem.", "Fall Dem. X VAP", 
                 "Fall Partisan", "Fall Partisan x VAP")
apsrtable(undervoteMMteach, undervoteMMparty, undervoteMMpartyB,
          caption = "Partial Pooling Model of School Board Undervote",
           model.names = c("Teacher Influence", "Party Influence", "Party Divide"),
            label = "tab:meUndv2", notes = list(stars.note), 
          Sweave = FALSE, coef.names = coefNamesUnd2)
@
\end{tiny}


Table \ref{tab:meUndv2} looks at interest group influence and how it affects 
voter roll-off. The results for the control variables are largely the same as 
in Table \ref{tab:meUndv1}, but here the undervote results are different than 
the results for total turnout. In all three cases I find evidence of group influence 
in the completion of spring ballots. In the case of partisan division, this effect 
is only present in larger communities -- as communities and division get larger, 
the turnout gap for school board races widens. 

In the case of teachers, as teachers make up a greater share of the electorate 
fewer voters cast a vote for school board -- paralleling the finding from above 
for overall turnout. Democratic party strength is also related with fewer voters 
completing the school board vote. An interaction effect just misses statistical 
significance here -- suggesting that as communities get larger and more Democratic, 
voter rolloff lessens. 




% Lagged turnout being higher, results in lower turnout differential
% higher fall turnout results in higher turnout differential
% bigger size = higher turnout differential
% whiter = higher turnout differential
% older - no difference
% more educated = higher turnout differential (more rolloff)
% higher income = lower turnout differential
% higher OOH share = higher voter rolloff
% imputation model dummy adjusts rolloff downward
% uncontested seat = higher rolloff
% less competitive seat = more rolloff, but only in smaller jurisdictions

\clearpage


