\label{turnoutRESULTS}
\begin{tabular}{| p{.25\textwidth} | p{.25\textwidth} | p{.1\textwidth} | p{.1\textwidth} | p{.1\textwidth} |} 
\hline
\textbf{Theory}  & \textbf{Evidence} & \textbf{Turnout?} & \textbf{Rolloff?} & \textbf{Magnitude}\\ \hline
Habit-forming voters  & Predictive lagged turnout & Yes & Yes & Moderate \\ \hline
Rational Voters & District size decreases turnout & Yes & Yes & Moderate \\ \hline
Strategic Voters & Small electorate and close election drives turnout up  & No & Yes  & Small \\ \hline
Teacher Influence &  Union membership as share of electorate drives up turnout & Some & Some & Small \\ \hline
Partisan Influence & Partisan division increases turnout & No & Some & Small \\ \hline
\end{tabular}
\end{small}
\end{center}
\end{table}

\subsection{Model Fit}

Assessing model fit in a mixed model framework is not as straightforward as with 
a standard OLS regression. I use a boostrapped estimate of the Root Mean Squared 
Error (RMSE) to assess how close the predicted values are to the fitted values across 
models. All of the models fit relatively well. Figure \ref{fig:modFitplot1} 
demonstrates how the multilevel model improves on the pooled fixed effect model 
fit by showing the original data, predicted values, and predicted values under 
the fixed effect model for the model of competitiveness predicting turnout.

<<modFitplot1, eval = TRUE, fig.cap="Comparing Model Fit for Competitiveness Model. Black represents observed value, purple represents fixed effect estimate, and the red dots are the estimate from the full model.">>=
library(broom)
plotdf <- augment(mixedModcloseC)
plotdf$yUnscale <- (plotdf$turnout * 2 * moddatScaleClose["turnout", 2]) + 
   moddatScaleClose["turnout", 1]
plotdf$yhatUnscale <- (plotdf$.fitted * 2 * moddatScaleClose["turnout", 2]) + 
   moddatScaleClose["turnout", 1]
plotdf$x1Unscale <- (plotdf$minBlaisLago * 2 * moddatScaleClose["minBlaisLago", 2]) + 
   moddatScaleClose["minBlaisLago", 1]
plotdf$yhatFixed <- (plotdf$.fixed * 2 * moddatScaleClose["turnout", 2]) + 
   moddatScaleClose["turnout", 1]

plotSlope <- coefficients(lm(yUnscale ~ x1Unscale, data = plotdf))[2]
plotInt <- coefficients(lm(yUnscale ~ x1Unscale, data = plotdf))[1]

cases <- sample(unique(plotdf$distid), 12)
plotdf <- plotdf[plotdf$distid %in% cases, ]

ggplot(plotdf, aes(x = year, y = yUnscale/100)) +
         geom_point() + 
  geom_point(aes(x = year, y = yhatFixed/100), color = I("purple")) +
  geom_point(aes(x = year, y = yhatUnscale/100), color = I("red")) + 
  facet_wrap(~distid) + 
  scale_y_continuous(label = percent) +
  theme_bw() + labs(x= "Year", y = "Turnout", title = "Comparing Model Fit")
@


<<modFitplot2, eval = FALSE>>=
plotdf <- augment(mixedMod1lagsb)
plotdf$yUnscale <- (plotdf$turnout * 2 * moddatScaleLag["turnout", 2]) + 
   moddatScaleLag["turnout", 1]
plotdf$yhatUnscale <- (plotdf$.fitted * 2 * moddatScaleLag["turnout", 2]) + 
   moddatScaleLag["turnout", 1]
plotdf$x1Unscale <- (plotdf$turnoutLag2 * 2 * moddatScaleLag["turnoutLag2", 2]) + 
   moddatScaleLag["turnoutLag2", 1]
plotdf$yhatFixed <- (plotdf$.fixed * 2 * moddatScaleLag["turnout", 2]) + 
   moddatScaleLag["turnout", 1]

plotdf$x1Unscale <- plotdf$x1Unscale * 100
plotdf <- plotdf[plotdf$x1Unscale < 99, ]

