rmsePlot2 <- ggplot(out, aes(x = mod, y = bootMed, ymax = bootMed + (bootSE *2), 
                ymin = bootMed - (bootSE *2))) + 
  geom_pointrange() + geom_point(aes(x = mod, y = original), color = I("red")) + 
  labs(x = "Model Name", y = "Model RMSE", title = "Rolloff Models") + 
  coord_flip() + theme_dpi()
@


\subsection{Substantive Effects}

After finding evidence in favor of voters behaving as theory might expect, I now 
turn my attention to exploring the magnitude of these effects. In order to estimate 
the substantive impact, I conduct simulations to show the change in the dependent 
variable holding all else constant and manipulating an independent variable of 
interest. While this type of simulation does not represent the interrelated 
nature of the independent variables in the models above, it does allow me to 
demonstrate the maximum possible effect predicted by the models described above -- 
assuming the independent variable is changing while all other variables remain 
constant.\footnote{Simulations are conducted using a modification of the \texttt{sim} 
function in the \texttt{arm} package for R.}


\subsubsection{Turnout}


<<fixefplot1, eval=FALSE, fig.cap="Coefficient Plot for Full Model Fixed Effects",fig.height=8.5, fig.width=8, results='hide'>>=
sim1 <- FEsim(mixedMod1lagsb, nsims = 1000); sim1$dv <- "Lags"
sim2 <- FEsim(mixedModcloseC, nsims = 1000); sim2$dv <- "Close"
sim3 <- FEsim(fullmodMMd, nsims = 1000); sim3$dv <- "Defeat"
sim4 <- FEsim(fullmodMMblq, nsims = 1000); sim4$dv <- "Competitiveness"

simPlot <- do.call(rbind, list(sim1, sim2, sim3, sim4))
simPlot$dv <- factor(simPlot$dv, levels = c("No Incumb.", "Contest", 
                                            "Defeat", "Competitiveness"))
alpha <- 1.96
simPlot$upr <- simPlot$meanEff + (alpha*simPlot$sdEff)
simPlot$lwr <- simPlot$meanEff - (alpha*simPlot$sdEff)

simPlot$color <- "Not Sig."
simPlot$color <- ifelse(simPlot$lwr >0 | simPlot$upr < 0, "Sig", "Not Sig.")

ggplot(simPlot, aes(x = variable, y = meanEff, ymax = upr, 
                    ymin = lwr, color = color)) + 
  scale_color_brewer(palette = "Dark2") + 
  geom_point() + geom_errorbar(h=1.5) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0, size = I(1.1), color = I("black"), linetype = 2, 
             alpha = I(0.6)) +
  coord_flip() + 
  facet_wrap(~dv) + 
  theme_dpi(base_size=12) +theme(legend.position = "bottom") + 
  labs(x = "Variable", y = "Effect", color = "Stat. Sig.")

# confint(fullmodMMni, method = "Wald", level = 0.9)
@

Figure \ref{fig:fixefSub1} shows the effect of four of the key variables of interest 
from the models above. These plots show four randomly sampled observations (represented 
by a different colored line) with the independent variable on the x-axis and the 
predicted school board turnout on the y-axis. Along the x-axis is a rug-plot showing 
the relative density of observations at each range of the x-variable to help demonstrate 
where the model may be extrapolating with less data. The two dashed lines shown 
represent the inter-quartile range or the predicted values from the model being 
simulated. This is presented to provide a reference for how powerful the effect 
of the independent variable -- as shown by the slope of the line -- is relative 
to the overall variability in turnout. 

In Figure \ref{fig:fixefSub1} two patterns stand out. First, the interaction 
effect for the teacher share of voters depicted in the top right panel, stands 
out immediately -- the differing slopes are the result of the variation in VAP 
across these randomly sampled observations. This effect is strong and is further 
explored in Figure \ref{fig:interactPlot1}. However, from this panel it is clear 
the effect of the teacher share of voters can move school board turnout a substantial 
degree of the range of the turnout variable -- as evidenced by the steep slope of 
some of these lines. 

The second pattern is in the bottom-left panel. As competitiveness decreases from 
left to right, turnout decreases substantially. Across the range of competitiveness 
the effect is almost equivalent to two quartiles of school board turnout. More 
practically, it results in turnout decreasing between three and five percentage 
points. Given that median turnout is around 20\%, this effect is quite large. 

On the other diagonal there is an expected relationship between prior turnout 
and current turnout as positive and fairly strong. Partisan division has a very 
small positive effect of turnout with more polarized communities having a slight 
increase in expected voter turnout -- but much less substantial than the effect 
of teacher voter share or competitiveness. 


