All of the models specified included random intercepts for both school districts 
and years as a way to control for unobservable characteristics of each district 
and secular change in turnout from spring election to spring election. Figure 
\ref{fig:randomEffectplot1} demonstrates the variability in the district intercepts 
for each of the four models. These plots can be interpreted as the deviation of 
the intercept of a school district from the average district. This figure arranges 
the estimate of the individual intercept for each school, and its corresponding 
95\% confidence interval, from lowest to highest to depict the variability in 
district intercepts. This is known as a caterpillar plot and is useful in demonstrating 
the variability among units in a mixed-effect model. 

The results in \ref{fig:randomEffectplot1} show large and consistent variance 
between the four models with a number of individual districts deviating from the 
average district even with their confidence intervals. The height of the points 
can be interpreted as the number of standard deviations in election turnout the 
school district deviates from the average for all districts in the model. 

