I now review the substantive impact of key independent variables on voter rolloff. 
Figure \ref{fig:teachUndEff} shows the variation in the impact of the teacher share 
of voters on voter rolloff across four quartiles of district VAP. For the smallest 
districts, the teacher share of voters does not affect voter rolloff -- voters 
complete their ballots at the same rate whether teachers comprise 1\% or 5\% of the 
electorate. 

As districts get larger, the impact of teachers grows stronger and in the second 
and third quartile, the impact of moving from 2\% to 3\% teachers in the VAP can 
increase voter rolloff in school board elections by nearly as much as the interquartile 
range for the variable. The simulation in the final panel, for the largest districts,
is unlikely as it is very unlikely that very large VAP district would ever have 5\% 
teachers. 

Figure \ref{fig:compUndEff} provides the same look at an interactive effect with 
district size, but for school board seat competitiveness. Competitiveness plays no 
role in voter turnout for smaller school districts, but as districts get larger, 
less competitive elections see much greater voter rolloff. 

<<teachUndEff, fig.cap="Interaction Effect of Teacher Share on Undervote">>=
p3
@

<<compUndEff, fig.cap="Interaction Effect of Competition on Undervote">>=
p4
@


