\section{Discussion}

I find strong evidence that, like voters in other elections, school board voters 
are highly habit-forming, appear to evaluate their voting decision somewhat rationally, 
and appear to be shaped by social group voting. It bears repeating that school board 
voters are a subset of the total electorate and the fall electorate in Wisconsin 
school districts, but it appears that they too appear to behave in accordance 
with voter theories. 

% Evidence for habit forming voters
% Evidence for rational voters
% Evidence for social group voters
% Evidence for discontent among voters

Table \ref{tab:meTurnout} provides strong evidence that 
in Wisconsin spring elections voters are habit forming. The electorate in these 
elections is smaller and highly autocorrelated. As the electorate size increases, 
the less prior turnout matters. This is exactly in line with the theoretical 
expectation that smaller electorates are more habit forming than larger electorates. 
Though school boards are experiencing much less voter turnout, they appear to be 
behaving within the expectations of other electorates.

Table \ref{tab:meClose} demonstrates that competitive races matter for turnout in
school board elections as well. Uncontested races have lower voter turnout than races 
with contestation. Little difference exists between races for open seats and races 
featuring an incumbent. Competitiveness matters as well, with races that are contested 
but include a non-competitive candidate having depressed turnout as well. This 
suggests that, despite a lack of campaigning by candidates or major media coverage, 
voters in school board elections are able to assess whether or not to vote based 
on an assessment of how contested a race will be. 

A school board specific effects were explored in Table \ref{tab:meInf} and Table 
\ref{tab:mePartisan}. In this, the larger the teacher share of voters in the district, 
the lower the voter turnout. This is in line with the theory of regulatory capture -- 
the nomination process being strongly controlled by the teacher union. 

This study is not without its limits. A first limitation is the question of how 
much spring election voter turnout can be considered to be driven by local 
down-ticket elections. This is particularly true during the period of study in 
this chapter, which included the highest turnout spring election in Wisconsin 
history -- a result that cannot be attributed to a slate of particularly 
contentious school board elections, but instead to the aftershocks of historical
partisan politics surrounding Governor Scott Walker and the state fiscal crisis. 
In an effort to address these concerns I confirmed the theory expectations on a 
narrower dependent variable -- the difference in turnout between the top spring 
ticket and the school board race. While this addresses the concern about falsely 
attributing larger spring election forces to school boards, more evidence directly 
about school boards would be preferred. 

Second, this study is an attempt to infer voter behavior from the observed patterns 
of turnout and rolloff observed over the last several years in a sample of 
Wisconsin school districts. Thus, while theories of habit-forming voters are in
accordance with the pattern observed here, this study is limited by its inability
to peer into the rationales of individual voters. In order to disentangle the many
confounding theories, future research must survey eligible voters and ask them about
their decision to vote or not vote in local school board elections like the work
of \citet{oliver12}. Thus, readers should be cautious in overinterpreting the
results here. 

Unlike the results in Chapter \ref{chap:candidacy}, the results in this chapter 
broadly reflect the theoretical expectations for elections. This suggests that 
although the school board electorate is different than the general election electorate, 
that electorate behaves in expected ways -- that is, habit forming, selective about 
their vote mattering, and influenced by social group mechanisms. 

\clearpage

